{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"9aa9920aceb2488a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is a `Public Subnet`?**|What is a `Public Subnet`?]]","x":-100,"y":0,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"fd7cfa2396231d94","type":"text","text":"A `public subnet` is a `subnet` that can have a direct route to an `Internet Gateway`.  \nExample route table:\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      Internet Gateway\n```\nThis means traffic going to the internet can go directly through the `Internet Gateway`.  \nA `public subnet` is commonly used for resources that need direct internet access, such as:\n```text\nLoad Balancer\nBastion Host\nPublic EC2 instance\nNAT Gateway\n```","x":350,"y":0,"width":600,"height":415},
		{"id":"832b0a234a374e42","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is a `Private Subnet`?**|What is a `Private Subnet`?]]","x":-100,"y":475,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"bc154eb04e921aa9","type":"text","text":"A `private subnet` is a `subnet` that does not have a direct route to an `Internet Gateway`.  \nExample private subnet route table:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\n```\nThis means resources in the `private subnet` can communicate inside the `VPC`, but they do not have direct internet access.  \nA `private subnet` is usually used for backend resources, such as:\n```text\nApplication server\nPrivate EC2 instance\nDatabase\nInternal service\n```\nThis is better for security because users from the internet cannot directly connect to those resources.  ","x":350,"y":475,"width":600,"height":450},
		{"id":"b9104dde05f37291","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is a `NAT Gateway`?**|What is a `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":985,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"fd131914c501cf73","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` is a `VPC` component that allows private resources to access the internet without allowing the internet to directly access them.  \nSimple idea:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 → NAT Gateway → Internet\n```\nThe private resource can start an outbound connection to the internet.  \nBut users from the internet cannot start a new inbound connection directly to that private resource.  ","x":350,"y":985,"width":600,"height":250},
		{"id":"4ec894224c3a6168","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**Why does a private `EC2` need a `NAT Gateway`?**|Why does a private `EC2` need a `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":1295,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"fd796f6b716cc2ab","type":"text","text":"Imagine you have an `EC2` instance in a `private subnet`.  \nBecause it is private, internet users cannot directly connect to it.  \nThat is good for security.  \nBut sometimes, that private `EC2` still needs to go out to the internet.  \nFor example, it may need to:\n```text\nDownload security updates\nInstall packages\nCall an external API\nConnect to some AWS services\nPull Docker images\nSend logs to external services\n```\nWithout a `NAT Gateway`, the private `EC2` may not be able to access the internet.  \n**How does a private `EC2` access the internet through a `NAT Gateway`?**  \nThe private `EC2` sends internet traffic to the `NAT Gateway`.  \nExample:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 → NAT Gateway → Ubuntu update server\n```\nThe `NAT Gateway` then sends the traffic to the internet on behalf of the private `EC2`.  \nWhen the internet server replies, the response comes back through the same `NAT Gateway`.\n```text\nUbuntu update server → NAT Gateway → Private EC2\n```\nThis reply is allowed because the private `EC2` started the connection first.  ","x":350,"y":1295,"width":600,"height":745},
		{"id":"395030669162ba4f","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**Why can the reply come back to the private `EC2`?**|Why can the reply come back to the private `EC2`?]]","x":-100,"y":2100,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"3154c99c3cb04582","type":"text","text":"The reply is allowed because it belongs to the same connection that the private `EC2` started.  \nExample:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 starts connection:\nPrivate EC2 → NAT Gateway → Ubuntu update server\nReply comes back:\nUbuntu update server → NAT Gateway → Private EC2\n```\nThe `NAT Gateway` remembers the outbound connection.  \nSo when the reply comes back, it knows which private `EC2` should receive the response.  ","x":350,"y":2100,"width":600,"height":330},
		{"id":"e66a728590e2926a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**Why can internet users not directly connect to private `EC2` through a `NAT Gateway`?**|Why can internet users not directly connect to private `EC2` through a `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":2490,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"3baf5cd6c4d19b2e","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` allows outbound internet access from private resources.  \nIt does not allow new inbound connections from the internet.  \nWrong idea:\n```text\nInternet user → NAT Gateway → Private EC2\n```\nThis is not how `NAT Gateway` is used.  \nCorrect idea:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 → NAT Gateway → Internet\nInternet reply → NAT Gateway → Private EC2\n```\nThe private `EC2` must start the connection first.  ","x":350,"y":2490,"width":600,"height":365},
		{"id":"084e51898ac40969","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is the difference between `Internet Gateway` and `NAT Gateway`?**|What is the difference between `Internet Gateway` and `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":2915,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"fa36955e1bdb8967","type":"text","text":"An `Internet Gateway` is used for direct internet access.  \nA `NAT Gateway` is used for private outbound internet access.\n```text\nPublic subnet:\n0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway\nPrivate subnet:\n0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway\n```\nSimple comparison:\n```text\nInternet Gateway = direct internet road\nNAT Gateway      = private outbound internet road\n```\nA resource using an `Internet Gateway` usually needs a `Public IP` or `Elastic IP`.  \nA resource using a `NAT Gateway` does not need a public IP.  ","x":350,"y":2915,"width":600,"height":390},
		{"id":"7e0ee4ef2bb1a100","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is a `NAT Instance`?**|What is a `NAT Instance`?]]","x":-100,"y":3365,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"756fc9e78ee11c9a","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Instance` is an `EC2` instance manually configured to perform `NAT`.  \nSimple idea:\n```text\nNAT Instance = EC2 instance used as a NAT device\n```\nYou manage the `NAT Instance` yourself.  \nThat means you are responsible for:\n```text\nOperating system updates\nSecurity patches\nInstance size\nHigh availability\nScaling\nSecurity Group configuration\nRoute table configuration\nFailure recovery\n```\nA `NAT Instance` can be cheaper for small learning projects, but it requires more manual work.  ","x":350,"y":3365,"width":600,"height":470},
		{"id":"e734e307354521ae","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is the difference between `NAT Gateway` and `NAT Instance`?**|What is the difference between `NAT Gateway` and `NAT Instance`?]]","x":-100,"y":3895,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"839b06d6a67e45e2","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` is managed by AWS.  \nA `NAT Instance` is managed by you.\n```text\nNAT Gateway:\nManaged by AWS\nHighly available inside one AZ\nEasier to use\nCosts more\nNAT Instance:\nManaged by you\nRuns on EC2\nMore control\nMore manual maintenance\n```\nFor learning or very small systems, a `NAT Instance` can be used to reduce cost.  \nFor production systems, a `NAT Gateway` is usually preferred because it is easier and more reliable.  ","x":350,"y":3895,"width":600,"height":455},
		{"id":"39d995e9c1f57586","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**Why is `NAT Gateway` not free?**|Why is `NAT Gateway` not free?]]","x":-100,"y":4410,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"0f1367e33501a22b","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` is not free.  \nAWS charges for it mainly in two ways:\n```text\n1. The time the NAT Gateway is running\n2. The amount of data that goes through it\n```\nSo even if your application is small, a running `NAT Gateway` can still create cost.  \nCorrected spelling from your note:\n```text\ntiem        → time\namoun tof   → amount of\nintenet     → internet\n```\nThe correct sentence is:\n```text\nFirst, you pay for the time the NAT Gateway is running.\nSecond, you pay for the amount of data that goes through it.\n```\n**How many `NAT Gateways` should a small system use?**  \nFor a small system, you might use one `NAT Gateway` to reduce cost.  \nExample:\n```text\nVPC\n├── Public Subnet\n│   └── NAT Gateway\n├── Private Subnet A\n│   └── Route to NAT Gateway\n└── Private Subnet B\n    └── Route to NAT Gateway\n```\nThis can work for learning or small projects.  \nBut it is not the best design for high availability.  \nIf the `Availability Zone` that contains the `NAT Gateway` has a problem, private resources depending on that `NAT Gateway` may lose outbound internet access.  \n**How many `NAT Gateways` should a production system use?**  \nFor a production system that needs high reliability, you usually deploy `NAT Gateways` in multiple `Availability Zones`.  \nExample:\n```text\nVPC\n├── AZ 1\n│   ├── Public Subnet 1\n│   │   └── NAT Gateway 1\n│   └── Private Subnet 1\n│       └── 0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway 1\n│\n└── AZ 2\n    ├── Public Subnet 2\n    │   └── NAT Gateway 2\n    └── Private Subnet 2\n        └── 0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway 2\n```\nThe reason is high availability.  \nIf one `Availability Zone` has a problem, resources in another `Availability Zone` can still use their own `NAT Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":4410,"width":600,"height":1435},
		{"id":"6a67efcedc059b76","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**Why should each `Availability Zone` have its own `NAT Gateway` in production?**|Why should each `Availability Zone` have its own `NAT Gateway` in production?]]","x":-100,"y":5905,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"16bb217ed2a32706","type":"text","text":"Each `Availability Zone` should have its own `NAT Gateway` in production because it improves reliability.  \nExample:\n```text\nPrivate Subnet in AZ 1 → NAT Gateway in AZ 1\nPrivate Subnet in AZ 2 → NAT Gateway in AZ 2\n```\nThis design avoids depending on only one `NAT Gateway`.  \nIt also avoids sending traffic across `Availability Zones` unnecessarily.  \nSimple idea:\n```text\nSmall system:\nOne NAT Gateway may be enough to reduce cost\nProduction system:\nOne NAT Gateway per AZ is better for high availability\n```","x":350,"y":5905,"width":600,"height":425},
		{"id":"6621b317a358f83e","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What do you need to create a `NAT Gateway`?**|What do you need to create a `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":6390,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"8d5f1b54d48da4b1","type":"text","text":"To create a `NAT Gateway`, you need three important things:\n```text\n1. Public subnet\n2. Elastic IP address\n3. Route table configuration\n```\nThe `NAT Gateway` must be placed in a `public subnet`.  \nIt also needs an `Elastic IP`.  \nThen private subnet route tables must send internet traffic to the `NAT Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":6390,"width":600,"height":270},
		{"id":"ff283c43dce28e0d","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**Why must a `NAT Gateway` be placed in a public subnet?**|Why must a `NAT Gateway` be placed in a public subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":6720,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"ff1136662cb68976","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` must be placed in a `public subnet` because it needs access to the internet.  \nThe public subnet route table should have a route like this:\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      Internet Gateway\n```\nThis allows the `NAT Gateway` to send traffic out to the internet.  \nSimple idea:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 → NAT Gateway in public subnet → Internet Gateway → Internet\n```\nThe private `EC2` does not directly use the `Internet Gateway`.  \nThe `NAT Gateway` uses the `Internet Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":6720,"width":600,"height":390},
		{"id":"71ad3743b340b745","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**Why does a `NAT Gateway` need an `Elastic IP`?**|Why does a `NAT Gateway` need an `Elastic IP`?]]","x":-100,"y":7170,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"f35527600dd482af","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` needs an `Elastic IP` because it needs a public IP address to send traffic to the internet on behalf of private resources.  \nExample:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 private IP: 10.0.2.10\nNAT Gateway Elastic IP: 54.20.30.40\n```\nWhen the private `EC2` accesses the internet, the internet sees the `Elastic IP` of the `NAT Gateway`.\n```text\nPrivate EC2 → NAT Gateway → Internet\nInternet sees source IP:\n54.20.30.40\n```\nThe private `EC2` itself still does not need a public IP.  ","x":350,"y":7170,"width":600,"height":390},
		{"id":"d9ec98588fa42d3b","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is the route table configuration for a private subnet using `NAT Gateway`?**|What is the route table configuration for a private subnet using `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":7620,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"c38240d889fd0e23","type":"text","text":"Private subnets need a route like this:\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      NAT Gateway\n```\nThis means:\n```text\nIf traffic wants to go outside the VPC,\nsend it to the NAT Gateway.\n```\nA more complete private subnet route table looks like this:\n```text\nDestination    Target\n10.0.0.0/16    local\n0.0.0.0/0      NAT Gateway\n```\nThe first route keeps internal `VPC` traffic inside the `VPC`.  \nThe second route sends internet traffic to the `NAT Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":7620,"width":600,"height":495},
		{"id":"f5019960685ba462","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is the full route design for public and private subnets?**|What is the full route design for public and private subnets?]]","x":-100,"y":8175,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"4272c51b5546322b","type":"text","text":"Example:\n```text\nVPC: 10.0.0.0/16\nPublic Subnet Route Table:\n10.0.0.0/16 → local\n0.0.0.0/0   → Internet Gateway\nPrivate Subnet Route Table:\n10.0.0.0/16 → local\n0.0.0.0/0   → NAT Gateway\n```\nThe `public subnet` has direct access to the `Internet Gateway`.  \nThe `private subnet` has no direct route to the `Internet Gateway`.  \nInstead, the `private subnet` sends internet traffic to the `NAT Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":8175,"width":600,"height":370},
		{"id":"f976daf7a0654414","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is the correct flow for private `EC2` internet access?**|What is the correct flow for private `EC2` internet access?]]","x":-100,"y":8605,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"2d2d0a8679416c67","type":"text","text":"Example:\n```text\nPrivate EC2: 10.0.2.10\nNAT Gateway Elastic IP: 54.20.30.40\n```\nOutbound flow:\n```text\nPrivate EC2\n→ Private subnet Route Table\n→ NAT Gateway\n→ Internet Gateway\n→ Internet\n```\nMore detailed version:\n```text\n10.0.2.10\n→ NAT Gateway\n→ 54.20.30.40\n→ Internet\n```\nThe internet sees the `Elastic IP` of the `NAT Gateway`, not the private IP of the `EC2`.  ","x":350,"y":8605,"width":600,"height":550},
		{"id":"a34ab574f356bd0e","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What happens when the internet server replies?**|What happens when the internet server replies?]]","x":-100,"y":9215,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"4cb00b7d6cba9d1c","type":"text","text":"Example connection:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 → NAT Gateway → Ubuntu update server\n```\nReply:\n```text\nUbuntu update server → NAT Gateway → Private EC2\n```\nThe reply is allowed because it is part of the same connection started by the private `EC2`.  \nThe `NAT Gateway` tracks the connection and sends the reply back to the correct private resource.  ","x":350,"y":9215,"width":600,"height":305},
		{"id":"292a7eb2e04adb0a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is an important detail about the `Elastic IP`?**|What is an important detail about the `Elastic IP`?]]","x":-100,"y":9580,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"89fe0d9fd6b1ed8a","type":"text","text":"The `Elastic IP` used by a `NAT Gateway` must not already be attached to another resource.  \nFor example, it must not already be attached to:\n```text\nEC2 instance\nNAT Instance\nAnother NAT Gateway\n```\nIf the `Elastic IP` is already attached to another resource, you must detach it first.  \nThen you can attach it to the `NAT Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":9580,"width":600,"height":280},
		{"id":"69d441d79f2f7a14","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**What is the corrected version of the `Elastic IP` note?**|What is the corrected version of the `Elastic IP` note?]]","x":-100,"y":9920,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"3f688f346ec751b4","type":"text","text":"Your note says:\n```text\nOne important detail is that the Elastic IP address must not already be attached to another resource.\nIf it is already connected to a NAT Instance or EC2 instance, you must detach it first.\n```\nThis is correct.  \nA cleaner version is:\n```text\nThe Elastic IP must be available.\nIt must not already be associated with another EC2 instance, NAT Instance, or NAT Gateway.\nIf it is already associated with another resource, detach it first.\n```\nThis is important because one `Elastic IP` can only be associated with one resource at a time.  ","x":350,"y":9920,"width":600,"height":430},
		{"id":"6aa2e1caf2245507","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/5. Checklist for NAT Gateway#**Why does a private `EC2` not need a public IP when using `NAT Gateway`?**|Why does a private `EC2` not need a public IP when using `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":10410,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1c11806e626e5568","type":"text","text":"A private `EC2` does not need a public IP because the `NAT Gateway` has the public IP.  \nExample:\n```text\nPrivate EC2:\nPrivate IP only\nNAT Gateway:\nElastic IP\n```\nThe private `EC2` sends traffic to the `NAT Gateway`.  \nThe `NAT Gateway` sends that traffic to the internet using its own `Elastic IP`.  \nSimple idea:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 uses NAT Gateway's public IP to go out\n```\n**Can a `NAT Gateway` replace an `Internet Gateway`?**  \nNo.  \nA `NAT Gateway` does not replace an `Internet Gateway`.  \nThe `NAT Gateway` itself still needs a route to the internet through an `Internet Gateway`.  \nCorrect design:\n```text\nPrivate EC2\n→ NAT Gateway\n→ Internet Gateway\n→ Internet\n```\nThe `NAT Gateway` is placed in a `public subnet`.  \nThat public subnet must have this route:\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      Internet Gateway\n```\nWithout the `Internet Gateway`, the `NAT Gateway` cannot send traffic to the internet.  \n**Can internet users connect directly to private `EC2` through `NAT Gateway`?**  \nNo.  \nInternet users cannot directly connect to private `EC2` through a `NAT Gateway`.  \nThis is blocked by design.  \nCorrect allowed flow:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 starts connection\nPrivate EC2 → NAT Gateway → Internet\n```\nCorrect reply flow:\n```text\nInternet server replies\nInternet → NAT Gateway → Private EC2\n```\nNot allowed:\n```text\nInternet user starts new connection\nInternet user → NAT Gateway → Private EC2\n```\nA `NAT Gateway` is for outbound access, not direct inbound access.  \n**Simple idea**  \nA `public subnet` can have a direct route to an `Internet Gateway`.  \nA `private subnet` has no direct route to an `Internet Gateway`.  \nA `NAT Gateway` allows private resources to access the internet safely.\n```text\nPublic subnet:\n0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway\nPrivate subnet:\n0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway\n```\nRemember it like this:\n```text\nInternet Gateway = direct internet access\nNAT Gateway      = private outbound internet access\nNAT Instance     = EC2 manually configured to do NAT\n```\nThe most important point:\n```text\nPrivate EC2 can start internet connection through NAT Gateway.\nInternet users cannot start direct connection to private EC2 through NAT Gateway.\n```","x":350,"y":10410,"width":600,"height":2135}
	],
	"edges":[
		{"id":"1208258c1c2af1af","fromNode":"9aa9920aceb2488a","fromSide":"right","toNode":"fd7cfa2396231d94","toSide":"left"},
